WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.060
Today I want to talk about the benefits
of a non-rules-based program of language

00:00:06.065 --> 00:00:07.410
learning and language instruction.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.650
The last video I made, I talked
about this book, "The Mind Within

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.940
the Net" by Manfred Spitzer, where
he talks about how the brain learns.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.560
And I drew some conclusions from
that, conclusions as to how the brain

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.120
learns, how the brain—based on
being bombarded with events, or with

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.590
words, or with structures, exposure
to the language, input—gradually

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.310
the brain develops its own rules
about how the language works.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.590
In other words, those
rules cannot be instructed.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:38.070
The rules are developed naturally
by the brain. And I talked about

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.760
how the hippocampus, which is the
short-term and medium-term memory,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.340
tries to get access to as many,

00:00:44.345 --> 00:00:48.605
say, events or words or facts or things
of that nature that it holds onto

00:00:48.605 --> 00:00:52.265
for a short period of time and then
gradually pumps it into the cortex.

00:00:52.295 --> 00:00:57.754
Now, there are some very important
benefits from this non-rules-based

00:00:57.754 --> 00:00:58.864
language learning approach.

00:00:58.864 --> 00:01:00.065
The first one is

00:01:00.065 --> 00:01:01.025
that we shouldn't doubt ourselves.

00:01:01.055 --> 00:01:05.825
I talked about how [for] children at play, it's
an opportunity for them to experiment

00:01:05.885 --> 00:01:08.705
in a relatively safe environment.

00:01:08.705 --> 00:01:10.925
There can be no catastrophic event.

00:01:10.925 --> 00:01:13.835
It's not like going hunting in the old
days with their parents where they might

00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:16.325
cause a major catastrophe to happen.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:19.414
It's safe, experiment,
interact with people.

00:01:19.455 --> 00:01:22.425
Gradually, through this
experience, acquiring knowledge.

00:01:22.455 --> 00:01:24.585
So that's how we learn languages.

00:01:24.585 --> 00:01:28.935
We learn languages through being
exposed, much like the child at play.

00:01:29.054 --> 00:01:31.215
In fact, we should be
like the child at play.

00:01:31.425 --> 00:01:34.425
So we are listening, we are
reading, and we are speaking.

00:01:34.664 --> 00:01:36.884
And when we are speaking, we shouldn't

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.100
doubt ourselves.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.410
We shouldn't try to remember a rule.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:41.610
"Is this correct?"

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:47.370
We should rely more on the natural process
of whatever the cortex or the long-term

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:52.230
acquisition of ability, knowledge, longer-
term knowledge, whatever state it is at.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.260
And whether what you retrieve
at this time is right or wrong,

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:56.700
none of that should matter.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.890
You should just go with your instinct.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.090
Don't second guess yourself anymore
than the child at play is going to

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:04.770
second guess him- or herself.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:05.850
Just go with it.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:06.530
I think we can

00:02:06.805 --> 00:02:08.395
just rely on the process.

00:02:08.575 --> 00:02:11.845
It doesn't really matter if you make
a mistake. You aren't going to do better

00:02:11.875 --> 00:02:16.945
by trying to refer to what Krashen
talks about as the affective filter.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.855
In other words, second guessing
yourself based on some rule.

00:02:19.915 --> 00:02:24.085
Just rely on whatever comes out.
Because it's a gradual process of

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.415
learning and you just have to go with
whatever you got. And you'll probably

00:02:27.415 --> 00:02:31.765
be better that way than if you
self-consciously try to refer to a rule.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.320
So don't doubt yourself.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:37.459
The second thing that I think is very
beneficial from this understanding of

00:02:37.459 --> 00:02:41.959
how language learning works is that
we should focus on being active. In

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:45.649
other words, rather than worrying about
what we have achieved in the language.

00:02:45.655 --> 00:02:49.519
And I think this message goes as much to
the learners who are always hoping that

00:02:49.519 --> 00:02:53.180
they would be better than they are, as
well as the teachers who want to teach

00:02:53.185 --> 00:02:53.239
and

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.580
test and show X points out of 10.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.500
The teacher can't teach you the rule.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.330
Testing you on the rule doesn't matter.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.650
You have to just rely on staying so
active that there are enough language

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.610
events, words, sounds, sentences,
exposure to the language coming at

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.540
you. And that the hippocampus then,
if we take that model, is then passing

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.740
on to the longer-term cortex area to
develop your skill in the language.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.430
As long as you keep doing that, the more
active you are, the more things are being

00:03:20.535 --> 00:03:24.435
pushed into your cortex in the sort of
longer-term processing area, the better.

00:03:24.435 --> 00:03:28.395
So all you need to worry about is not how
well you've done on the last test or how

00:03:28.395 --> 00:03:30.015
well you're going to do on the next test.

00:03:30.015 --> 00:03:31.725
You just have to keep active.

00:03:31.725 --> 00:03:33.885
So that simplifies the task at hand.

00:03:33.915 --> 00:03:37.515
So if you can go out and find interesting
things to do in the language or vary

00:03:37.525 --> 00:03:42.655
between interesting but difficult texts
and easier texts where there's perhaps

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.305
more repetition of familiar words.

00:03:44.335 --> 00:03:47.425
You keep doing this kind of stuff,
pumping it into your cortex.

00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:50.395
Eventually you're going to
improve. But you improve slowly.

00:03:50.485 --> 00:03:55.015
The third thing that's beneficial about
understanding this way of learning

00:03:55.245 --> 00:03:59.055
is something that I have observed on
many occasions, and that is that if you

00:03:59.055 --> 00:04:02.655
have had enough activity in the language,
have done enough listening and reading,

00:04:02.685 --> 00:04:07.125
have read books and so forth, if you
then leave the language for a while, when

00:04:07.125 --> 00:04:11.130
you go back to it, because everything
is stored in your long-term memory.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:14.370
All of that stuff is still
there somewhere in the cortex.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:18.120
You may not be able to retrieve it
right away, but as you reactivate the

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:23.370
language, you reactivate all of these
associations that were part of how you

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.770
acquired the words. Associations with

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.030
sounds, with context, with similar
words or different words, or

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.799
whatever those associations were.

00:04:31.799 --> 00:04:37.500
There's this network of associations
that connect all these words in some way.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:41.010
And so even if you leave the language,
if you've got it to a sufficient

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.450
level, you don't have to worry.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.320
And in fact, as I have said, I have
often found that when I go back to

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.480
a language that I have left, in what
I termed a period of "benign neglect"

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.969
of course, in the short term, I
forget certain words. But in the

00:04:52.969 --> 00:04:54.980
long-term, I am better than ever.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:05:00.050
So I am better today in even those
languages that I speak quite well,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.230
but which I didn't spend much
time on because I was learning

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.670
new languages. But when I go back to
my Japanese, or my Chinese, or my French,

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:12.440
or my Spanish, I'm actually better
now than I was before because all of

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:17.060
that activity somehow has improved
my ability to retrieve the language,

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:20.870
use the language in ways that I don't
fully understand. But I am convinced

00:05:21.145 --> 00:05:24.415
if we take the position that we
are not going to learn rules and

00:05:24.415 --> 00:05:28.555
extrapolate from rules, but instead
we're going to expose ourselves to as

00:05:28.555 --> 00:05:32.065
much of the language as possible,
hopefully in interesting contexts,

00:05:32.065 --> 00:05:35.725
but as long as we're exposed all the time
and we're not doubting ourselves when we

00:05:35.725 --> 00:05:39.925
use the language, we're like the child at
play, and we stay as active as we possibly

00:05:39.925 --> 00:05:42.295
can, pumping stuff into our cortex,

00:05:42.295 --> 00:05:45.835
then after an absence and we go back
to that language, we're going to find

00:05:45.850 --> 00:05:49.270
that we are at least as good as
we ever were, and possibly better.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:53.140
So there's lots of advantages
to taking this non-rules-based

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.520
approach to language learning.

